WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.840 --> 00:00:02.240
- [Bertie] Only minutes old,

00:00:02.320 --> 00:00:04.340
this harp seal pup is quick to

00:00:04.500 --> 00:00:07.240
fall in love with its icy home sweet home.

00:00:08.540 --> 00:00:10.480
The ice melts.

00:00:10.880 --> 00:00:13.120
In just 10 days time, mom will leave

00:00:13.120 --> 00:00:15.700
her behind and never look back.

00:00:16.180 --> 00:00:18.020
The countdown begins.

00:00:18.020 --> 00:00:19.120
(baby seal cooing)

00:00:19.120 --> 00:00:21.760
10, put on at least four pounds per day.

00:00:21.760 --> 00:00:23.980
Nine, learn to swim before the ice melts.

00:00:23.980 --> 00:00:26.620
Eight, don't panic there
is no time for this.

00:00:26.620 --> 00:00:27.460
Seven,

00:00:27.480 --> 00:00:28.100
six,

00:00:28.100 --> 00:00:29.240
five, four,

00:00:29.240 --> 00:00:30.220
three,

00:00:30.220 --> 00:00:31.440
two, one.

00:00:35.860 --> 00:00:39.240
(wind blowing)

00:00:39.240 --> 00:00:40.280
(daunting music)

00:00:40.340 --> 00:00:44.000
At first glance the arctic
feel brutally devoid

00:00:44.040 --> 00:00:45.320
of life.

00:00:45.400 --> 00:00:46.920
Though a select group of animals have

00:00:46.960 --> 00:00:49.200
figured out a way to thrive.

00:00:49.320 --> 00:00:50.640
(seal pup howls)

00:00:51.660 --> 00:00:55.100
But the real force here
works quietly underfoot.

00:00:56.020 --> 00:00:59.540
Every winter sea ice
starts small and delicate.

00:00:59.540 --> 00:01:02.560
Then it grows over
billions of square miles.

00:01:02.580 --> 00:01:05.320
How is it possible that
this freeze up, holds the

00:01:05.320 --> 00:01:07.500
key to our entire planet?

00:01:07.760 --> 00:01:10.440
Like a lung, sea ice
breathes life into the arctic

00:01:10.440 --> 00:01:11.920
every year.

00:01:11.920 --> 00:01:15.260
In a cycle that impacts the
climate across the globe.

00:01:16.040 --> 00:01:19.080
This is the story from the
edge of the Canadian arctic

00:01:19.080 --> 00:01:20.460
where it all starts.

00:01:22.520 --> 00:01:24.160
My name is Bertie Gregory and I am a

00:01:24.160 --> 00:01:27.280
National Geographic Wildlife Filmmaker.

00:01:27.460 --> 00:01:30.620
Everything is at stake, we are all waiting

00:01:30.620 --> 00:01:32.420
for the big freeze.

00:01:36.060 --> 00:01:39.300
(clock ticking)

00:01:39.360 --> 00:01:41.780
(calm music)

00:01:43.440 --> 00:01:46.780
Every year, harp seals
leave the rich waters of

00:01:46.860 --> 00:01:48.580
the northern Hudson Bay

00:01:48.600 --> 00:01:49.840
and join thousands of

00:01:49.840 --> 00:01:53.760
other migrating south
out of polar bear range

00:01:53.860 --> 00:01:55.600
to their breeding oasis.

00:01:57.400 --> 00:01:58.940
A massive ice flow in the

00:01:58.980 --> 00:01:59.760
Gulf of St.

00:01:59.760 --> 00:02:00.640
Lawrence.

00:02:00.640 --> 00:02:06.100
(flute music)

00:02:06.100 --> 00:02:08.240
(snow crunching)

00:02:08.240 --> 00:02:09.760
It is hard to believe right now,

00:02:09.760 --> 00:02:11.800
that we are walking over the ocean.

00:02:11.800 --> 00:02:15.560
Everywhere you look as
far as you can see is ice.

00:02:15.880 --> 00:02:17.500
That is the reason why I
have got this incredibly

00:02:17.500 --> 00:02:20.980
sexy and very fashionable
outfit on and a stick, is 'cause

00:02:20.980 --> 00:02:23.000
we want to make sure we
don't fall through a crack in

00:02:23.000 --> 00:02:25.720
the ice, into underneath us
which is hundreds of feet

00:02:25.720 --> 00:02:27.580
of freezing cold water.

00:02:27.580 --> 00:02:29.640
But right now we are looking for what is

00:02:29.640 --> 00:02:34.260
arguably the cutest animal on the planet.

00:02:34.780 --> 00:02:36.540
(seal pup cries) And I can hear it.

00:02:37.240 --> 00:02:43.300
(flute music)

00:02:43.300 --> 00:02:47.560
This beautiful little animal,
hello, is a harp seal pup.

00:02:47.560 --> 00:02:51.160
It was born here on the ice
about four or five days ago

00:02:51.160 --> 00:02:52.780
judging by its size.

00:02:53.000 --> 00:02:55.180
It is totally helpless without mom

00:02:55.180 --> 00:02:56.280
right now.

00:02:56.340 --> 00:03:00.680
(water bubbling) (flute music)

00:03:00.680 --> 00:03:03.340
(seal chirping)

00:03:19.820 --> 00:03:21.680
In a colony of thousands,

00:03:21.680 --> 00:03:24.480
mom needs to make sure she
is not feeding the wrong pup.

00:03:29.540 --> 00:03:31.580
Here she comes.

00:03:31.680 --> 00:03:32.560
The kiss,

00:03:32.640 --> 00:03:34.140
this what harp seals are

00:03:34.140 --> 00:03:35.240
famous for,

00:03:35.240 --> 00:03:38.060
that kiss is how the mom and pup recognize

00:03:38.060 --> 00:03:38.980
each other.

00:03:39.160 --> 00:03:41.740
(seals cooing)

00:03:47.420 --> 00:03:48.820
This is what that little pup has been

00:03:48.820 --> 00:03:50.300
waiting for.

00:03:50.360 --> 00:03:51.620
It is dinner time

00:03:53.500 --> 00:03:54.600
and it has really

00:03:54.600 --> 00:03:57.000
got to make the most of these
feeding sessions because

00:03:57.000 --> 00:03:58.840
its has only got 10 days with its mom.

00:03:58.840 --> 00:04:01.700
After those 10 days, the female
is going to stop suckling

00:04:01.700 --> 00:04:04.460
the pup, and she is going to
head north with the males.

00:04:04.460 --> 00:04:06.560
That little pup is going to
be abandoned with nothing

00:04:06.560 --> 00:04:07.420
to eat.

00:04:07.580 --> 00:04:10.280
So it has really got to build
up its fat reserves now.

00:04:18.680 --> 00:04:20.320
Since filming that pup,

00:04:20.320 --> 00:04:21.480
I have found something that is

00:04:21.480 --> 00:04:22.840
a little bit gross.

00:04:22.840 --> 00:04:25.660
So you see down here,
there is this blood trail.

00:04:26.620 --> 00:04:28.860
It leads to this.

00:04:28.880 --> 00:04:30.960
Now this looks like death

00:04:30.960 --> 00:04:31.580
but

00:04:31.580 --> 00:04:33.840
it is not, this is life,
this is the placenta.

00:04:33.840 --> 00:04:36.320
But it is not actually frozen yet.

00:04:37.120 --> 00:04:39.740
That means that somewhere around here,

00:04:39.740 --> 00:04:41.020
is a new born pup

00:04:41.060 --> 00:04:42.820
that has literally just been born.

00:04:42.820 --> 00:04:44.220
I mean that would take

00:04:44.220 --> 00:04:48.140
less than an hour to freeze,
so somewhere here, is

00:04:48.160 --> 00:04:49.000
that little pup.

00:04:57.520 --> 00:04:59.040
Look, there it is.

00:05:01.140 --> 00:05:03.880
Yeah, that one is just a few minutes old.

00:05:03.900 --> 00:05:05.060
Look how yellow it is

00:05:06.480 --> 00:05:07.180
and you can still see

00:05:07.180 --> 00:05:09.140
it has got its umbilical cord attached.

00:05:10.860 --> 00:05:12.860
You know compared to that
other pup we had which was

00:05:12.860 --> 00:05:15.980
only a few days old, four
or five days older, this

00:05:15.980 --> 00:05:19.440
shows just how fast they grow

00:05:19.500 --> 00:05:20.920
and this little pup

00:05:20.920 --> 00:05:23.020
has definitely got some catching up to do.

00:05:25.140 --> 00:05:31.920
(tribal music)

00:05:32.280 --> 00:05:34.140
You know it is really cool
to see such a young pup

00:05:34.180 --> 00:05:36.720
but it is actually not a good thing.

00:05:36.720 --> 00:05:39.880
This one is four or five
days behind the rest and

00:05:39.980 --> 00:05:42.860
these adults are already
in a race against time

00:05:43.120 --> 00:05:45.480
to raise their pups before
the ice breaks up so

00:05:45.660 --> 00:05:49.220
this one has an extra big challenge ahead.

00:05:49.560 --> 00:05:53.900
(violin music)

00:05:54.080 --> 00:05:58.140
(seal pup whimpers)

00:05:58.140 --> 00:06:00.600
(violin music) [Bertie]
Our changing climate is

00:06:00.600 --> 00:06:03.580
making early ice breakups
more and more common.

00:06:03.580 --> 00:06:04.540
In five of the

00:06:04.680 --> 00:06:06.200
past ten years

00:06:06.200 --> 00:06:08.340
bad ice meant the colony failed to

00:06:08.640 --> 00:06:09.680
raise pups.

00:06:11.560 --> 00:06:13.160
In 2017

00:06:13.160 --> 00:06:14.720
the ice broke up so early

00:06:14.840 --> 00:06:16.660
that the entire colony

00:06:17.000 --> 00:06:19.280
of pups drowned overnight.

00:06:19.460 --> 00:06:28.280
(fast violin music)

00:06:40.740 --> 00:06:44.440
So you can see what was
once a giant solid sheet is

00:06:44.520 --> 00:06:48.300
now breaking up and the ice
is really on the move, you

00:06:48.440 --> 00:06:49.280
can see it.

00:06:50.240 --> 00:06:53.260
Here now, it is the time for
these pups to learn to swim

00:06:53.340 --> 00:06:55.800
because soon these chunks
are going to get smaller and

00:06:55.800 --> 00:06:59.540
smaller and uh, and yeah
they will be forced to swim.

00:06:59.680 --> 00:07:02.660
So it is swimming time they got to go.

00:07:03.920 --> 00:07:06.280
And that is exactly
what is on this mothers

00:07:06.280 --> 00:07:07.360
mind right now.

00:07:09.420 --> 00:07:14.000
This real chunky monkey is
so fat it is unbelievable

00:07:14.000 --> 00:07:16.240
the amount of change
just in a few days from

00:07:16.280 --> 00:07:19.880
that tiny little new born,
now to a big fat sausage.

00:07:20.820 --> 00:07:23.000
So we are going to hang out
and see if mom encourages it

00:07:23.000 --> 00:07:25.380
for its first swimming lesson.

00:07:25.380 --> 00:07:31.740
(cello music)

00:07:31.740 --> 00:07:34.580
(seal pup cooing)

00:07:40.600 --> 00:07:46.280
(seal pup whimpers)

00:07:46.500 --> 00:07:47.260
Are we good to go?

00:07:47.360 --> 00:07:48.160
- [Cameraman] Yeah

00:07:50.040 --> 00:08:02.740
(flute music)

00:08:02.740 --> 00:08:06.600
(seal pup cries)

00:08:07.740 --> 00:08:10.500
(seal pup cries)

00:08:15.480 --> 00:08:17.660
- [Bertie] Look how she
is using her giant belly

00:08:17.760 --> 00:08:18.760
as a big swim float

00:08:18.840 --> 00:08:19.900
to help her pup breathe.

00:08:21.120 --> 00:08:24.480
You can see she is so
protective of it in the water.

00:08:24.520 --> 00:08:27.020
And anytime any of the
other adults come anywhere

00:08:27.020 --> 00:08:30.440
near her she chases them
off so aggressively.

00:08:40.360 --> 00:08:43.380
It is really doing it, its swimming.

00:08:44.220 --> 00:08:45.520
Look at it go.

00:08:45.600 --> 00:08:50.020
(flute music)

00:08:53.720 --> 00:08:56.120
How incredible must it be to realize

00:08:56.120 --> 00:08:58.720
you are actually built for the very medium

00:08:58.720 --> 00:09:01.160
that was so daunting just minutes ago.

00:09:11.820 --> 00:09:14.520
Now that the mother knows
her pup has the skills to

00:09:14.520 --> 00:09:17.380
survive the ice breakup,
it is time for the

00:09:17.560 --> 00:09:18.900
hardest lesson of all.

00:09:20.020 --> 00:09:21.320
Saying goodbye.

00:09:25.400 --> 00:09:28.160
(seal pup howls)

00:09:29.800 --> 00:09:32.320
The male harp seals are
now gathering nearby.

00:09:32.320 --> 00:09:34.960
Ready to mate with the females.

00:09:35.340 --> 00:09:36.240
Okay, getting in.

00:09:45.960 --> 00:09:49.000
It is time for the adults to
join and head to the north.

00:09:50.320 --> 00:09:53.020
(seals chirping)

00:09:53.020 --> 00:09:57.500
(violin music)

00:09:57.620 --> 00:10:00.140
We have met truly extraordinary animals on

00:10:00.160 --> 00:10:01.060
this adventure.

00:10:01.060 --> 00:10:02.460
surrounded by the pack.

00:10:02.580 --> 00:10:05.740
Their lives governed by
ticking clocks getting them

00:10:05.880 --> 00:10:09.580
to run further, fly faster or swim deeper.

00:10:12.220 --> 00:10:15.540
But the thing that ties
them and us together is the

00:10:15.760 --> 00:10:17.600
planets loudest ticking clock.

00:10:18.460 --> 00:10:19.920
The arctic sea ice.

00:10:23.820 --> 00:10:26.640
The big freeze used to give
us millions of square miles of

00:10:26.640 --> 00:10:31.180
sun reflecting surface,
stabilizing the earth's climate.

00:10:31.220 --> 00:10:34.340
Over just the last thirty
years, we have lost more

00:10:34.340 --> 00:10:36.900
than a third of that sea ice.

00:10:36.920 --> 00:10:39.500
Now rather than reflecting the suns heat,

00:10:39.660 --> 00:10:41.740
open water absorbs it.

00:10:41.800 --> 00:10:44.940
And we are witnessing its deadly effects.

00:10:45.100 --> 00:10:47.300
(violin music)

00:10:47.480 --> 00:10:48.660
But there is hope

00:10:48.740 --> 00:10:51.800
and it lies with harnessing
clean energy, using our

00:10:51.800 --> 00:10:54.980
land more efficiently and reforesting.

00:10:54.980 --> 00:10:58.880
Saving polar bears go beyond
just a nice thing to do,

00:10:58.880 --> 00:11:00.840
we are also saving ourselves.

00:11:02.420 --> 00:11:05.140
It really is in our hands
whether we want this place to

00:11:05.260 --> 00:11:06.280
exist in the future.

00:11:06.280 --> 00:11:08.980
Now is really a turning point.

00:11:08.980 --> 00:11:10.640
Do we let it go or

00:11:10.640 --> 00:11:12.760
do we really band together
to make sure that it

00:11:12.800 --> 00:11:14.360
survives into the next century?

00:11:14.560 --> 00:11:33.020
(orchestral music)

00:11:36.000 --> 00:12:09.540
(piano music)

